{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure node1 to audit changes to /etc/inventory.conf using the key inventory-config.","status":"satisfied","summary":"auditd is active and the live and persistent rule exactly targets /etc/inventory.conf with write/attribute auditing and key inventory-config. The rule remained loaded after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bd326370019c4d38","cmd-a2439b8659094268","cmd-600dd3c572b744f6"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Record both successful and unsuccessful change attempts in the system audit log.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Controlled tests produced a successful root write and a denied nobody write. The audit log contains corresponding success=yes and success=no/EACCES records for the exact file under inventory-config, including fresh records after reboot; test content was restored.","evidence_ids":["cmd-abc3b2c6e5ed46f4","cmd-7e00f8d3daf04586","cmd-3634783a9afd49c8","cmd-714b84d4a189404a"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The package-index refresh and auditd installation were necessary prerequisites (cmd-ed360e84354e4b4a, cmd-34e9ffa8c2c34687); rule creation/loading implemented the outcome (cmd-bd326370019c4d38); both file mutations were bounded verification tests whose original content hash was restored (cmd-abc3b2c6e5ed46f4, cmd-714b84d4a189404a); and the scheduled reboot served the mandatory restart protocol (cmd-564eb236cbca4554). Package health remained sound, no new failed service appeared, and lifecycle differences were expected reboot effects.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ed360e84354e4b4a","cmd-34e9ffa8c2c34687","cmd-bd326370019c4d38","cmd-abc3b2c6e5ed46f4","cmd-564eb236cbca4554","cmd-714b84d4a189404a","global:after_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task is fully satisfied. Direct audit records demonstrate both successful and denied changes under the required key. Restart evidence shows a pre-restart healthy audit workload, reboot acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, recovered auditd/rule/file state, and fresh post-reboot audit behavior.","limitations":["Node-wide system state was reported as degraded because ssh.service was already failed before execution and remained failed afterward. Thus restart evidence proves recovery of the audit workload but not an entirely clean global system state (cmd-3fc821327eaf4d8d, global:after_prepare:node1:failed-services, global:after_executor:node1:failed-services)."]}
